// The operand position, and the reason it is not the same fix as the hand-off: `rejectMixedOperands`
// bails on any spelling it does not recognize as a number, and its own comment names the escapee —
// "`string + string`, a bool, AN ENUM". For `==` that silence is not a missing niggle. These two enums
// have DIFFERENT widths and different variant names, and the comparison below evaluated TRUE: `A::A2`
// and `W::W2` are both 2, so the program took a branch the source never offers.
//
// kama's comparison model is otherwise a contract (`==` -> Equatable.equals), which is what makes a raw
// C integer comparison sneaking in underneath it worth its own fixture.
type enum A : uint8  { A0, A1, A2 }
type enum W : uint32 { W0, W1, W2 }

fn int32 main()
{
    A a = A::A2;
    W w = W::W2;
    bool eq = (a == w);
    return 0;
}
